{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological survival is non-normative and retrospective; scientific preference is prospective and normative.","Legal due process aims for justice (a value); Popper's method aims only for error-elimination (a negative criterion)."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that 'survival of tests' correlates with 'truth' independent of induction.","If a coherent definition of verisimilitude is restored that avoids the Tichý/Miller paradox."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on elimination of failures rather than confirmation of success.","Preference for the current survivor does not guarantee future survival.","Normative rule (choose the survivor) is distinct from descriptive outcome (the survivor exists)."],"source_memory_refs":["biological_natural_selection_survival_of_fittest","legal_procedural_rationality_due_process"],"transfer_claims":["Just as betting on the undefeated boxer is rational not because they will win, but because they haven't lost yet, preferring the best-corroborated theory is rational only as a adherence to the rule of testing.","The lack of guarantee in natural selection mirrors the lack of predictive certainty in Popperian science."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a methodological rule of 'critical rationality' rather than an inductive inference of probability, but this defense costs the scientist any claim to predictive reliability or truth-likeness (verisimilitude) in the strong sense, reducing the preference to a pragmatic gamble without epistemic justification.","counterconditions":["If the preference implies a higher probability of future success, it smuggles in induction.","If Popper's concept of verisimilitude is logically incoherent (per Tichý/Miller), the appeal to 'truth-likeness' as a justification collapses.","If the distinction between descriptive survival (biology) and normative choice (science) cannot be bridged without inductive assumptions."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument distinguishes between the context of discovery (where induction is rejected) and the context of action (where methodological rules apply). It posits that preferring the best-tested theory is not a prediction that it will succeed (which would be inductive), but a commitment to the rule that has so far survived the most severe criticism. The mechanism relies on the asymmetry of falsification: while we cannot know a theory is true, we know which theories have failed. Choosing the one that hasn't failed yet is the only rational move available, even if it offers no guarantee of future success.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}